Subject: [PATCH] exynos990: keep the bootstrap TTBR0 while bringing the VM up

Marks 38, 39 and 41 are all written, mark 10 is not, and the only code
between them is set_mmu_ttb(invalid_ttep) followed by flush_mmu_tlb().
After that flush the very next store (the mark byte itself) never
returns, and no synchronous-exception byte is left either - so the first
access after the flush either faults in a way the early handler cannot
record, or points at tables the early walk cannot use.

Leave the bootstrap TTBR0 in place for now and label mark 10 honestly.
If the kernel then walks past it into pmap_bootstrap, the switch is the
culprit and the mapping cpu_ttep installs for the kernel image needs to
be understood before the switch comes back; if it still stops, the
problem is elsewhere in that window.

diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c b/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
--- a/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
@@ -2316,7 +2316,9 @@
-	set_mmu_ttb(invalid_ttep & TTBR_BADDR_MASK);
+	/* bring-up: the switch is deferred until the tables are proven */
+	if (0)
+		set_mmu_ttb(invalid_ttep & TTBR_BADDR_MASK);
 
 	flush_mmu_tlb();
-	sweet_early_mark(10, "TTBR0 off; TLB flushed");
+	sweet_early_mark(10, "TTBR0 kept; TLB flushed");
